lear value bet or a desperation play. Given the board and the betting action, I can only lose.  It's brutal, but I *must* fold. The math is clear.\n\n\n", 'output': 'Evaluation: My hand is 4s 9h. The board is 7d 6h Ac 7s 6s. My best 5-card combination is the board itself: 7-7-6-6-A (Two Pair with Ace kicker). I cannot beat any hand. I can only split the pot if the opponent also cannot beat the board (i.e., has no 6, no 7, and no pocket pair 88 or higher). The opponent has moved All-in for nearly 20,000 chips into a very small pot (likely just blinds).\nIf I call, I risk ~19,900 chips.\n- If I lose (Opponent has 6, 7, 88+), I lose 19,900.\n- If I tie (Opponent plays board), I get my stack back plus half the tiny existing pot (approx +50 to +100 chips).\nThe risk (19,900) is massive compared to the reward (half a blind). Even if the probability of the opponent having a winning hand is low, the pot odds are terrible. I can never win the pot outright, only chop. Therefore, folding is the only correct play to preserve capital.\n\nReason: I am playing the board and can only tie or lose. Calling an All-in risks my entire stack for a negligible gain (splitting a tiny pot). The risk-to-reward ratio is extremely poor.\n\nAnswer: -1'}
