ong hand like a Flush (three clubs are on the board), Trips (Jack), or a strong Pocket Pair, as they likely would have bet earlier to build the pot or protect their hand.\n\nIn a limped and checked-down pot, a Pair with a King kicker is often the winning hand. Betting here has little upside: worse hands (like Queen-high or lower) will likely fold, meaning you don't get paid, and better hands (like an Ace-high, a Flush, or a pair of 2s/5s/8s) will call or raise. Checking is the optimal strategy to get to the showdown cheaply. You likely have the best hand, and checking avoids the risk of being raised or betting into a trapped monster.\n\nReason: The action has been checked down to the river, indicating weakness from the opponent. Your hand (Pair of Jacks, King kicker) has good showdown value in this specific scenario. Betting turns your hand into a bluff that forces out hands you already beat, while checking allows you to win the pot against weaker holdings without risking additional chips.\n\nAnswer: 0"}
